WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.770
The show must go on, even when your first
choice for the lead in your biopic doesn't

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:05.260
work out.

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:09.920
Queen drummer Roger Taylor recently offered
a few thoughts as to why Sacha Baron Cohen,

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:14.690
who was once set to play Freddie Mercury in
Bohemian Rhapsody, ended up being let go from

00:00:14.690 --> 00:00:15.300
the project.

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:19.940
Cohen, the English comedian and performer
known for his movies Borat and Bruno, as well

00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:25.290
as the TV shows Da Ali G Show and Who Is America,
was confirmed in 2010 to be taking on the

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:28.980
role of Freddie Mercury in the Queen bio-pic
Bohemian Rhapsody.

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:34.040
In a recent Associated Press interview, Taylor
delved into details as to why Cohen, who bears

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:39.020
a remarkable physical resemblance to Mercury,
ended up departing the project in 2013.

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:43.500
Queen guitarist Brian May, as well as Cohen
himself, have previously stated that Cohen's

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:48.360
exit amounted to differences of opinion over
exactly what type of film Bohemian Rhapsody

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:49.000
would be.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:53.820
Apparently, Cohen and Queen didn't see eye
to eye, a major problem, considering the legendary

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:56.320
rock band had the final say over the script.

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:00.860
To hear Taylor explain it, the film may have
turned out very differently if Cohen's artistic

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:03.580
whims had been allowed to dictate the course
of the project.

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:08.060
The musician went so far as to insinuate that
the fracture was largely Cohen's fault.

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:09.060
As Taylor put it,

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:10.840
"There was a lot of talk about Sacha and stuff.

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:11.999
It was never really on.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:15.800
I don't think he took it seriously enough,
didn't take Freddie seriously enough."

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:20.600
A 2016 interview with Cohen on The Howard
Stern Show seems to illustrate Cohen's side

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:25.310
of the story, with the comic seeming far more
interested in portraying the wild, debauched

00:01:25.310 --> 00:01:29.880
side of Mercury, as opposed to the legendary
vocalist's career highlights and significant

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:30.680
relationships.

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:34.940
"There are amazing stories about Freddie Mercury.  Crazy. Crazy.

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:39.840
"the guy was wild.   he was living  an extreme lifestyle."

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:45.259
Despite foreseeing possible conflicts between
his vision and the band's desire to "protect

00:01:45.259 --> 00:01:49.840
their legacy", and avoid an R rating, Cohen
plugged away on the project for years, pitching

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:52.759
potential screenwriters and directors to the
band.

00:01:52.759 --> 00:01:56.920
One major sticking point, which Cohen also
related in his Howard Stern interview, was

00:01:56.920 --> 00:02:01.039
that in his initial meeting with Queen, an
unnamed band member asserted that Mercury's

00:02:01.039 --> 00:02:04.819
death would actually occur in the middle of
the film, and that the second half of the

00:02:04.819 --> 00:02:08.459
movie would explore how the band regrouped
and carried on without him.

00:02:08.459 --> 00:02:13.710
It's a prospect Cohen found ridiculous, considering
Queen's largely retrospective post-Mercury

00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:14.200
career.

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:18.599
Amusingly, even after getting rid of Cohen,
the finished movie doesn't feature Freddie's

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:20.680
death, not in the middle, nor anywhere else.

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:25.060
So this supposed sticking point seems to have
turned out not to matter much at all.

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:29.069
Queen guitarist Brian May previously made
comments around the time of Cohen's initial

00:02:29.069 --> 00:02:33.950
firing, which also seem to line up nicely
with Taylor's assessment of the situation.

00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:35.890
Speaking with Rolling Stone, May said,

00:02:35.890 --> 00:02:40.090
"He makes outstanding performances, but they're
very much Sacha Baron Cohen performances.

00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:43.290
And we thought there has to be no distraction
in the Freddie movie.

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:47.250
You have to really suspend that disbelief,
the man who plays Freddie, you have to really

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:48.560
believe is Freddie.

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:50.879
And we didn't think that could really happen
with Sacha."

00:02:50.879 --> 00:02:55.019
According to the Queen bandmates' version
of events, it seems that Cohen's outsized

00:02:55.019 --> 00:03:00.010
personality was somehow incompatible with
the role of the famously flamboyant Mercury.

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:04.659
In that sense, while Cohen's interpretation
may have been just fine for a lurid, R-rated

00:03:04.659 --> 00:03:08.790
tell-all, it just wouldn't have been appropriate
for the more straightforward, near-mythical

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:13.700
retelling of Mercury's story that the singer's
surviving bandmates were intent on realizing.

00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:18.920
Fortunately, the bandmates found their Mercury
with the casting of Mr. Robot actor Rami Malek,

00:03:18.939 --> 00:03:22.099
who has already drawn widespread critical
acclaim for his portrayal.

00:03:22.099 --> 00:03:25.400
Taylor, for his part, is over the moon about
Malek's casting.

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:27.060
As he told the Associated Press,

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:28.060
"He's amazing.

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:29.299
Thank God we found him.

00:03:29.299 --> 00:03:31.579
He's just an incredible guy, in every way.

00:03:31.579 --> 00:03:35.659
He's lovely to be around, he's dedicated utterly,
and he's incredibly talented.

00:03:35.659 --> 00:03:36.780
[...] We couldn't have done better."

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:39.590
Audiences seem to agree that Malek's nailed
the part.

00:03:39.590 --> 00:03:43.969
In its first week of release in the U.K.,
Bohemian Rhapsody made a huge impression,

00:03:43.969 --> 00:03:48.750
beating out the likes of Halloween and A Star
is Born to claim the top spot at the box office,

00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:52.140
and American moviegoers won't have to wait
long to see what the fuss is all about.

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:55.219
Is the finished product a killer queen, or
a sheer heart attack?

00:03:55.219 --> 00:03:58.150
That's for you to decide, but at least the
band sounds happy.

